{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-budgeting", "file": "references/palette-selection-from-demos.md", "content": "# Separating palette mapping from target-color selection

When demos show a two-color recoloring, do not infer the selector solely from one example. A frequent hypothesis is that the majority/background color is changed into the minority/marker color while the minority cells become zero; a competing rule may instead select a fixed input color, or invert a global palette mapping. Compare exact frequencies and color identities across demos with different palettes and marker densities. Include degenerate or sparse examples, but recognize their limits: a single exceptional cell can establish how a marker is treated while leaving frequency-based selection ambiguous. Verify the proposed selector and operation over every complete pair, not merely the colors or a few cells.

A useful hypothesis ledger records for each demo: dimensions, palette, per-color counts, proposed selector, cell mapping, and exact equality status. If a new example contradicts the current density rule, retire it rather than silently changing mappings or hard-coding that palette. Only save a recurring transform once all known pairs pass exactly.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544541/attempts/31275a2022b64460844e75da5175e698/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-budgeting/references/palette-selection-from-demos.md"}

